            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house has a lot of air leakage coming from various areas:



** The adjoining wall and ceiling sections for the various additions of this house - mostly from the rear converted porch area



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** The front door leaks alot, as do some of the windows that are twisted in their frames from settling of the house. (the slider window on the driveway side of the converted porch is a good example).



** Completing a full air barrier wrap of the house when you insulate the walls will help also.



Savings: I modeled a modest 20% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 466 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 19.6 GJ. For your house, you may even achieve twice this amount and savings !!



There are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction, unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate; achieving a 20% reduction increases the rebate to $750, and a 30%+ reduction achieves the maximum rebate of $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

There are many sections and additions on your house, and I summarized them in a previous email as:

1. The original house on the left side = 2x4 walls with R12

2. The right addition = 2x6 walls with R20

3. The front entrance = 2x6 walls with R20

4. The tank room in the front left corner = 2x6 walls with R20

5. The rear converted porch area = 2x4 walls with R12



You are planning an exterior retrofit of your house and would like it to all come together as one house. For R12 walls, I usually recommend adding R15 - R20 and for R20 walls, I usually recommend adding R10 - R15. As you want the exterior to all have the same depth &amp; profile, I would recommend adding R15 (eg: 3" of extruded polystyrene foam board insulation) to 100% of all the walls.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). These rebates are pro-rated based on the amount of wall area that is upgraded.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls of your house by R15, you could potentially save $ 614 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

There are various ages &amp; types of windows in your house. Some older wood double pane windows, some double pane vinyl slider or casement windows, and even some triple pane vinyl windows. There are some obvious ones you want to replace, and then there are a few of the double pane windows that have "twisted" in their frames due to house settling and they now do not close very well.



I have modeled the option for you to replace 6 of them (you can choose to do up to 12 if you want) to ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend a full frame replacement window that is a triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By upgrading 6 of the older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 85 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.6 GJ and qualify for a $ 250 rebate per window (6 windows = $1,500) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. As mentioned above, you could choose to replace up to 12 windows for the maximum rebate of $3,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Please see the previous section on heating, as I am looking at a combination boiler system to provide both your heating and domestic hot water. The Energy Factor on your current Bosch water heater is 0.69, and the new combination boiler is estimated to increase this to 0.87 (or a 26% increase in overall efficiency). </HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a Blaze King Chinook CK24 (77% efficiency) and 2 small wall furnace heaters (65% - 75% efficiency). Based on the current wall insulation and air leakage, my software program indicates that all 3 heaters combined would struggle to keep the house warm on the coldest "design heat load" temperature of -41.6C. In fact, although your Blaze King is rated at 27,200 BTU for it's "Higher Heating Value" output, the computer software over rides this with a 72,000 BTU requirement that the house currently needs (you will see this figure on Page 2 of the Homeowner Information Sheet report !!!



This situation can be resolved in many ways:

     - add more insulation

     - reduce air leakage

     - look at some higher efficiency equipment



My report tackles all three of these !!



For this section of the report, I'm going to think outside the box and try to eliminate both wall furnaces and turn your wood stove into a supplementary system rather than your primary. You can obviously still use the woodstove as much as you want, however you would have the option of not needing to for a period of time if you so choose.



I am thinking towards a high efficiency propane "combination" boiler @ 95% AFUE that provides both heating &amp; domestic hot water. From the boiler, you can run a few thermostatically controlled heat zones:

     - one for the back bedroom

     - another for the front bathroom area (and the 2 adjacent bedrooms)

     - a third for the central part of the house (the woodstove can override this zone)



In addition, the boiler would also heat your hot water at about 25% higher efficiency than the current Bosch system.



Yes, a condensing boiler has "condensate" from the high efficiency combustion process, and most of the installations I see have a condensate neutralizer filtration system incorporated into the pump. Depending on the PH level of the condensate exiting the pump, this water may be adequate for using to supply your toilet (reducing the water requirements from what is hauled in from the lake). I think an average condensing heating appliance (boiler or furnace) in Yellowknife can produce about 5 - 10 gallons of condensate per full heating day. Not exactly sure how this would work, but something fun to talk over with your heating/plumbing contractor.



Savings: If you were to use this 95% AFUE combination propane boiler for all of your heating &amp; hot water needs, you could potentially save $ 1964 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 100.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,250 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



NOTE: For the savings ............ please look at the November 2019 fuel cost section of this report. For the wood, we have 2 rates - commercially delivered at $420 per cord or self-harvested at $100 per cord (cost of chain saw, truck, gas, beer, etc). My calculations use the commercial delivered rate of wood. Fuel costs are always changing &amp; I'm aware that both oil &amp; propane are way down this year (so far) - so I'm not going to get too hung up on fuel costs &amp; primarily deal with heating system efficiency &amp; ability to meet the heat load requirements of the house.

 

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $12,750 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $5,000 for the wall insulation

     - $1,500 for 6 main floor windows (can qualify for up to $3,000 for 12 window replacements if you want) 

     - $2,500 for the combination boiler heating &amp; hot water system

     - $   750 for a 20% air leakage reduction ($1,000 if you achieve a 30% reduction)

     - $3,000 for the bonus rebate (perhaps a $4,000 bonus rebate if you exceed a 50% reduction it total energy use)



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the heating system rebate, however it would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ECM circulation pumps for heating zones off the new boiler - 50% up to $175 each (5 maximum)

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In off-grid locations outside Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood does not vent outside and there is no bathroom fan, so it is just the clothes dryer that vents outside), indicated a pressure drop of 0.2 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current woodstove vents up a chimney &amp; does not have a dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe .......... so it is susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - A clothes dryer



Your house currently has a significant amount of natural air leakage and does not depend on mechanical exhaust ventilation. However, as you tighten up the house during your renovations, I'm sure you will be wanting some additional spot ventilation to manage indoor air quality and excess humidity:

        - Having the range hood fan vent to the outside so it can be used to vent cooking byproducts (steam, smoke, odours)

        - Having a bathroom exhaust fan to vent excess shower humidity &amp; bathroom odours



You should consider installing a bathroom exhaust fan, please choose an ENERGY STAR rated fan that use little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife - Ingraham Trail Off Grid



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings for off-grid locations along Ingraham Trail outside Yellowknife:

Hybrid Solar + Generator Electricity - $0.75 per kWh

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $0.68

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



